These functions have been optimized to be $x-y$ separable. The basis functions for steerability are not unique. For instance, figures @fig-steer_quad_basis (a and c) show two basis functions that span all rotations of $g_{xx}$. Figure (a) has separable filters corresponding to $g_{xx}$, $g_{xy}$ and $g_{yy}$, and figure (c) shows three rotated versions of $g_{xx}$ at 0, 60, and 120 degrees. Figure (c) is a non-separable basis spanning the same space as the filters of figure (a). Figures (b and d) show two basis functions that span all rotations on $h_{xx}$ spanning the same space as the filters of figure (b). Spatial scaling of the filters will result in changing $\sigma$.

![(a) Second derivative of Gaussian, $x-y$
separable steerable basis set. b) Approximation to Hilbert transform of second derivative of  Gaussian, $x-y$ steerable basis set. (c)  Nonseparable basis equivalent to (a). (d) Nonseparable basis set equivalent to (b).](figures/spatial_filter_sets/steer_quad_basis.png){width=100% #fig-steer_quad_basis}
